    {
      "text": "%spark\n//Imagine we are preparing a report and we want to see one total for each store. The SUM() aggregate function can help us with this. \n//The results are aggregated by each unique combination of year and store.\nspark.sql(\"\"\"\nselect \nsum(payment_amount) as totalPayment,\nyear(payment_date) as payment_year,\nstore_id as store\nfrom payment\ngroup by year(payment_date), store_id\norder by year(payment_date),store_id\n\"\"\").show()\n\n/*\nHowever, we can’t see the total payments by year: the total payments for 2018, 2019, 2020, or 2021. We cannot see the totals by store either, which would be a useful metric to have. Using GROUPING SETS allows us to view these totals.\n*/",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "ea04484ee84a4d2480bde"
    },
    {
      "text": "#### Top 2 salaries for each department",
