    },
    {
      "text": "####Window Functions\n[spark.apache.org](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html)\n\n[medium.com](https://medium.com/@erkansirin/spark-sql-window-functions-303702c194a8)\n>A group-by takes data, and every row can go only into one grouping. \nWindow functions operate on a group of rows, referred to as a window, and calculate a return value for each row based on the group of rows. Window functions are useful for processing tasks such as calculating a moving average, computing a cumulative statistic, or accessing the value of rows given the relative position of the current row.\n\n```\n\n1. Ranking Functions\n\nSyntax: RANK | DENSE_RANK | PERCENT_RANK | NTILE | ROW_NUMBER\n2. Analytic Functions\n\nSyntax: CUME_DIST | LAG | LEAD | NTH_VALUE | FIRST_VALUE | LAST_VALUE\n3. Aggregate Functions\n\nSyntax: MAX | MIN | COUNT | SUM | AVG | .\nBasic syntax\n\nMAX(salary) over(partition by dept) as dept_max_salary\n\n```",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "8c47481ee77f4f569da4c"
    },
    {
      "text": "\nunpivote df\n\n```\ndf.selectExpr(“row_label_column“, “stack(, , , , …)”)\n\n    row_label_column: Here in our case Country is row_label_column.\n    n_columns: No of Columns. Here it is 4\n    col0_label: Column Name\n    col0_value: Column Value\n```\n",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "af90afa728eb416fb8d0a"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nimport org.apache.spark.sql.expressions.Window\n\nval windowSpec  = Window.partitionBy(\"department\").orderBy(\"salary\")\n",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "a583e9794fd04f8d917ad"
    },
    {
      "text": "\n**In addition to working with any type of values, Spark also allows us to create the following\ngroupings types:**\n\n1. The simplest grouping is to just summarize a complete DataFrame by performing an\naggregation in a select statement.\n\n2. A “group by” allows you to specify one or more keys as well as one or more\naggregation functions to transform the value columns.\n\n3. A “window” gives you the ability to specify one or more keys as well as one or more\naggregation functions to transform the value columns. However, the rows input to the\nfunction are somehow related to the current row.\n\n4. A “grouping set,” which you can use to aggregate at multiple different levels. Grouping\nsets are available as a primitive in SQL and via rollups and cubes in DataFrames.\n\n5. A “rollup” makes it possible for you to specify one or more keys as well as one or more\naggregation functions to transform the value columns, which will be summarized\nhierarchically.\n\n6. A “cube” allows you to specify one or more keys as well as one or more aggregation\nfunctions to transform the value columns, which will be summarized across all\ncombinations of columns.\n\n\n>Each grouping returns a RelationalGroupedDataset on which we specify our aggregations.",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "b9809de4c70841a5a71d1"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\ndf.withColumn(\"lag\", lag(\"salary\", 1,0).over(Window.partitionBy(\"department\").orderBy(col(\"salary\"))))\n .withColumn(\"lead\", lead(\"salary\", 1, 0).over(Window.partitionBy(\"department\").orderBy(col(\"salary\"))))\n .withColumn(\"lag_diff\", col(\"salary\") - col(\"lag\"))\n .withColumn(\"lead_diff\", col(\"salary\") - col(\"lead\"))\n .show(false)\n\n /*\n F.lag(\"salary\", 1, 0) ve LAG(salary, 1, 0): 1 indicates how many rows to look up or down, and 0 the default value. If we don’t use default value, null value comes when there is no top or bottom row.\n */",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "8ff0338e699242d0a1798"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\n",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
