          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "9ce6bab3ef3e407aabd56"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\ndf.select(f.countDistinct(\"StockCode\")).show() //see spark optimizaiton chapter file for how spark does plan count distinct in background",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "d2d98e1be49f4c1180a86"
    },
    {
      "text": "\n1. Row number(rn) sorts all rows in each window sequentially.\n\n2.  rank, on the other hand, gives the same sequence number to employees with the same salary, but picks up where the row number(rn) left off for the next employee.\n\n3. dense_rank, unlike rank, gives the consecutive number,not the row number, to those who follow the ones with the same salary\n\n4. ntile : ntile is a function that takes integer arguments. According to these n integers determined by the user, it divides the window into n and assigns numbers to each group in a sequential manner.\n ",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "c33b759bb89f408fb332e"
    },
    {
      "text": "####Grouping sets\n>only available in sql, \n>GROUPING SETS are groups, or sets, of columns by which rows can be grouped together. Instead of writing multiple queries and combining the results with a UNION, you can simply use GROUPING SETS.\n\nGROUPING SETS in SQL can be considered an extension of the GROUP BY clause. It allows you to define multiple grouping sets in the same query.\n\n```\nSELECT\n    aggregate_function(column_1)\n    column_2,\n    column_3,\nFROM\n    table_name\nGROUP BY\n    GROUPING SETS (\n        (column_2, column_3),\n        (column_2),\n        (column_3),\n        ()\n);\n```",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "23b98f85b6364884ac6aa"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nval cubedf = spark.sql(\"\"\"\nwith temp as(select payment_amount as paymentAmount, year(payment_date) as payementYear, store_id as storeId from payment)\nSELECT\nsum(paymentAmount),\npayementYear,\nstoreid\nfrom temp\ngroup by cube(payementyear, storeid)\norder by payementyear, storeid\n\"\"\")\n\ncubedf.show()",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "daf3d12cff98438b8b02a"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\ndf.select(\n          f.first(\"description\"), \n          f.last(\"description\") , \n          f.min(\"quantity\"),\n          f.max(\"quantity\"),\n          f.sum(\"quantity\"), \n          f.sum_distinct(f.col(\"quantity\")),\n          f.avg(f.col(\"quantity\")),\n          f.mean(f.col(\"quantity\"))\n         )\n  .show(false)",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "141c5cd1d6af45ef8be64"
    },
    {
      "text": "####Window Aggregate Functions\n>how to calculate sum, min, max for each department using Spark SQL Aggregate window functions and WindowSpec. When working with Aggregate functions, we don’t need to use order by clause.",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "560bafa5119a4d62b6e72"
    },
    {
      "text": "#### Grouping\n\n>First we specify the column(s) on which we would like to\ngroup, and then we specify the aggregation(s). The first step returns a\nRelationalGroupedDataset, and the second step returns a DataFrame",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "3ec1d14e067e4639b39b0"
    },
    {
      "text": "#### Grouping Metadata\n\n~\n1. total payment all year, irespecive of year and storeid  --> gid => 3\n2. total payemnt for each year, irrespecive of storeid  --> gid => 1\n3. total payment for each  store year wise --> gid => 0\n4. cube df also include total payement by each store, irrespecive of years  ==>  gid ==> 2\n\n",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "4b65c9fc8cef4bbebed49"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nspark.sql(\"\"\"\nwith temp as(select payment_amount as paymentAmount, year(payment_date) as payementYear, store_id as storeId from payment)\nSELECT\n  SUM(paymentAmount),\n  payementYear,\n  storeId\nFROM temp\nGROUP BY  grouping sets(payementYear, storeId) \nORDER BY payementYear, storeId;\n\"\"\").show()\n//so, it's running two query ..as above cell",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "40bc62f137944163a817f"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\ndisplay(payment_date)",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
