      },
      "id": "0924496bd85345d691490"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nval rolupdf = spark.sql(\"\"\"\nwith temp as(select payment_amount as paymentAmount, year(payment_date) as payementYear, store_id as storeId from payment)\nSELECT\nsum(paymentAmount),\npayementYear,\nstoreid\nfrom temp\ngroup by rollup(payementyear, storeid)\norder by payementyear, storeid\n\"\"\")\nrolupdf.show()",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "d1c799a7c2bf4b0a9c236"
    },
    {
      "text": "#### Rollups\n>Similar to GROUPING SETS, you can use the ROLLUP option in a single query to generate multiple grouping sets.\n>ROLLUP assumes a hierarchy among the input columns. For example, if the input columns are:\nGROUP BY ROLLUP(column_1,column_2)\n\n```the hierarchy for this is column_1 > column_2, and ROLLUP generates the following grouping sets:\n(column_1, column_2)\n(column_1)\n()\n```\n\n>ROLLUP generates all grouping sets that make sense in this hierarchy. It generates a subtotal row every time the value of column_1 changes; this is the hierarchy we have provided. For this reason, we often use ROLLUP to generate subtotals and grand totals in reporting. The ordering of your columns in ROLLUP is very important.\n\nLet's look at a query that uses ROLLUP",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "1159d5241a1e425ba16f7"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nval windowSpecAgg  = Window.partitionBy(\"department\")\n\nval aggDF = df.withColumn(\"row\",row_number.over(windowSpec))\n    .withColumn(\"avg\", avg(col(\"salary\")).over(windowSpecAgg))\n    .withColumn(\"sum\", sum(col(\"salary\")).over(windowSpecAgg))\n    .withColumn(\"min\", min(col(\"salary\")).over(windowSpecAgg))\n    .withColumn(\"max\", max(col(\"salary\")).over(windowSpecAgg))\n    // .where(col(\"row\")===1).select(\"department\",\"avg\",\"sum\",\"min\",\"max\")\n    .show()",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "scala",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "62537fd01b664355981ce"
    },
    {
      "text": "#### cube\n\n>The CUBE is like combining GROUPING SETS and ROLLUP. It shows the detailed output of both.",
      "config": {
